starting this weekend in the german  alpine resort of el mao leaders of the  g7 will meet  they've invited extra guests to  participate including indian prime  minister modi underscoring the country's  growing geostrategic role in the world  here at dw we've been working on a  series looking at india's rise it's  called india's dilemma and you'll find  it on our dw news youtube page here's a  quick look  india finds itself in a dramatic  geopolitical squeeze  russia its old friend and arm supplier  is deepening ties with china  weakened by the war and becoming  subservient to a power that's no friend  to india  there are serious concerns in new delhi  about whether russia will continue to be  a reliable supplier  at the same time china's threat to india  becomes ever more apparent  compelling india to reinforce its  borders against attempts to take its  territory  the chinese are breathing down our  borders and they just killed our  soldiers less than two years ago  delhi faces deep dilemmas over how close  to get to the west  the us has had many many alliances not  all of them have fared very well  india has a long tradition of avoiding  close alliances and preferring to stay  non-aligned but the war is forcing it to  think again about all of its  relationships  and face up to some choices  it hasn't had to make before  in part one of this video we'll look at  how durable india's old friendship with  russia really is  and whether it can survive moscow's new  path  the russia relationship is now in a  position  where it really does need to be  rethought in part two we'll see how  tensions between india and china  are far more dangerous than the rest of  the world realizes  think about this  beijing even says that it should control  an entire indian state  after russia's invasion of ukraine  it's all too easy to imagine a  devastating war  so we are talking about  a face big section of the world a very  important part of the world are very  large armies nuclear armed countries  fighting fighting for each other  and in part three we'll ask whether  india and the west can really come  together  or if they're ultimately heading in very  different directions  when china attacked us  where was this degree of outrage from  europe or from other countries india is  coming to resemble russia and china far  more than it resembles western  democracies so as the world enters this  dangerous new era  india could be the deciding factor so no  matter where you are in the world the  choices made here in delhi  matati be sure to go online and catch  the full series and we have richard  walker chief international editor and  the series correspondent with us um  richard let's talk about india and this  upcoming g7 summit and i want to start  by playing a clip from u.s president joe  biden  i think that the relationship between  india and the united states  the largest democracies in the world  is destined to be stronger  closer  and tighter and i think he can benefit  the whole world  right and then i want to play a clip  from shashi tha or indian  parliamentarian and former diplomat  would i want india to publicly declare  itself an ally of a country i'm not even  sure i'm ready to bite that bullet  because  the word alliance  implies to some degree a mortgaging of  your freedom to make your own decisions  so it sounds like the two countries are  talking past each other yeah it can  sometimes seem like that melissa i mean  you certainly get a strong sense from  the americans and on my recent trip to  delhi uh i got this very strongly that  they would like to tie india closer into  the kind of western-led sort of what  they see as an alliance kind of liberal  democracies um you know which is really  sort of girding up for this sort of  pushback against the autocracies of  china and russia  and you do often get this sense that  india is sort of going along with it to  some extent but playing almost like hard  to get in some ways  and there are historical reasons for  that like india has this very long  history since independence of  non-alignment not wanting to throw all  of its eggs in one basket with any one  country so trying to sort of stress in  what the kind of language they talk  about now is wanting like the world to  be multi-polar that they don't want to  kind of get dragged into a sort of new  cold war uh sort of scenario but the  americans certainly trying to coax them  in with you know formats like the quad  you know this regional grouping in asia  with the americans with japan uh with  australia and india trying to sort of  say india you're a big democracy you  belong with us okay so they're part of  the quad uh but in terms of russia  though how  uh  to what extent will the leaders of the  g7 be able to convince india to reorient  its policy well certainly india's been  under a lot of pressure already to try  to put some distance between itself and  russia over the the war uh that russia  has launched in ukraine um india has  really pretty steady or right from the  beginning being clear that or trying to  take what they see is a kind of  principled independent position of  saying that um they do they call for a  ceasefire that they condemn violence um  but not really coming out and really  really making a break with russia for  instance at the united nations uh in the  united nations security council just  after the war began um they famously uh  did not vote against the russians  they abstained right um and  the indian message back on russia to  some extent to the west is sort of just  don't lecture us  um and there are various different  angles to that partly you know people  will say well it's the west that's been  starting wars in the last 20 years what  are you uh lecturing us about um  what's so different about this one also  on energy uh with you know western  countries trying to tell india don't  start buying russian energy while we're  trying to to sanction it and the indians  say well we have a very poor population  we owe it to our population to get  energy as cheaply as we can  uh and there are some criticisms that  actually or or observations that india  is actually  much more closer politically  with russia and china yeah well this is  the other side of the story because on  the one hand the west is very keen to  bind india closer on the other hand  they're concerned about what's happening  in india  about the illiberal direction that  indian democracy is moving in and we  spoke to some critical voices also as  part of this film while we were in india  and maybe we can queue up a a sound bite  from hacho singh bal he's the political  editor of a prominent kind of  intellectual news magazine there  india is coming to resemble russia and  china far more than it resembles western  democracies so the west will have to  deal with this country through very  hard-nosed means rather than by thinking  that this common shared value of  constitutional democracy is going to  pull india  into our sphere that is not true  yeah so what achar singh baal is saying  there is that this this idea of the west  that is trying to appeal to india to  join this kind of democratic pushback of  sort on values basis against china and  russia  that that's not going to work because  india is maybe not moving in in this  kind of liberal direction that that  india has for instance there's very  severe criticism of india's treatment of  its muslim minorities  and  other concerns about the direction that  indian democracy is taking so very  interesting to see how this dynamic  between india and the west plays out  richard walker thank you so much for  joining us in the studio  you
 

